## What Problem This Solves

Fixes an issue where editing a configured hook transform could leave the gateway running the old transform function after hooks hot reload rebuilt the mapping config.

## Why This Change Was Made

Hook mapping resolution now builds a candidate runtime snapshot without mutating the process-local transform cache. The transform cache generation is advanced only after the gateway accepts the new hooks runtime config at startup or after hot reload successfully resolves the candidate hooks config and reaches the runtime commit edge. A delayed import from an older generation can still finish its own delivery, but it no longer republishes into the current cache after an accepted reload, and a rejected candidate reload leaves the active transform cache untouched.

## User Impact

Operators can update hook transform files and have subsequent matching webhook deliveries use the edited transform without restarting the gateway.

## Evidence

- Production module boundary: `pnpm exec tsx ../../outputs/local-hook-transform-reload/proof-live.ts`

```text
$ pnpm exec tsx ../../outputs/local-hook-transform-reload/proof-live.ts
first-transform: before
after-rejected-candidate: before
after-reload: after
```

- `node scripts/run-vitest.mjs src/gateway/hooks-mapping.test.ts`: 31 passed, including rejected-candidate and overlapping-import regressions.
- `node scripts/run-vitest.mjs src/gateway/config-reload.test.ts`: 132 passed.
- `node scripts/run-vitest.mjs src/gateway/server-reload-handlers.test.ts`: 98 passed, including invalid hooks reload preserving the active transform cache.
- `node scripts/run-vitest.mjs src/gateway/server-runtime-config.test.ts`: 30 passed.
- `git diff --check`: passed.

